1️⃣ JavaScript Automatically Converts Objects (Coercion)

JavaScript is dynamically typed, which means:

👉 When you use an object in a context where a number or string is expected,
👉 JavaScript tries to convert the object automatically.

This process is called:

🔥 Type Coercion

Example from your code:

obj1 + obj2


Here:

obj1 and obj2 are objects

+ operator expects numbers or strings

So JavaScript asks:

“How do I convert these objects?”

2️⃣ valueOf() – How Objects Become Numbers
🔑 Rule:

When JavaScript needs a number, it calls:

object.valueOf()

Your implementation:
ArrayWrapper.prototype.valueOf = function() {
    return this.nums.reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0);
}


So when this runs:

obj1 + obj2


Internally JavaScript does:

obj1.valueOf() + obj2.valueOf()


Which becomes:

3 + 7 = 10


✅ That’s why:

obj1 + obj2; // 10


💡 Key takeaway
You are controlling how an object behaves in numeric operations.

3️⃣ toString() – How Objects Become Strings
🔑 Rule:

When JavaScript needs a string, it calls:

object.toString()


Your implementation:

ArrayWrapper.prototype.toString = function() {
    return "[" + this.nums.join(",") + "]";
}


So when you do:

String(obj1);


JavaScript does:

obj1.toString();


Result:

"[1,2]"


✅ That’s why:

String(obj1); // "[1,2]"

4️⃣ Operator + is SMART (and Confusing 😄)

The + operator behaves differently depending on context:

Situation	Behavior
number + number	Addition
string + string	Concatenation
object + object	Try valueOf()
string + object	Use toString()

In your case:

obj1 + obj2


Since both objects can become numbers (valueOf() exists),
JavaScript performs numeric addition.

🔥 If valueOf() was missing, JS would fall back to toString()!

5️⃣ Prototype-Based Programming (Very Important)

You added methods using:

ArrayWrapper.prototype.valueOf = function() { ... }
ArrayWrapper.prototype.toString = function() { ... }


This means:

All instances of ArrayWrapper

Share the same behavior

Memory-efficient

Classic JavaScript OOP

💡 This is true JavaScript inheritance, not class-based like Java/C++.

